Z||000005||Raydar||08-12-2001||04:49 PM||||$50000 budget? In Fiero terms, that pretty much means "cost is no object".<P>1. 88 Fiero (model doesn't matter since you're replacing everything, anyway.) GTs have the fastback body and are better optioned, as a rule. There are exceptions, though. GT and Formula have upgraded shock/strut valving and possibly different spring rates. Also a rear sway bar. All can be added to a base chassis.<BR>2. Northstar engine conversion. <BR>3. Held motorsports suspension and brakes.<P>I suggested an 88 as a starting platform because the entire suspension was redesigned that year. Parts are somewhat harder to find, and more expensive, though.<P>You *will* probably get as many opinions on your question as there are members on the Forum. Good luck.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||08-12-2001||04:57 PM||||Would it be feasible to strip the frame down, and start replacing members with lighter materials to cut weight? Tube arms? Tubular eng. cradle? That'll stiffen things up, and reduce weight.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000007||qwikgta||08-12-2001||05:01 PM||||Its been said, for that $$ the sky is the limit, go with an 88, V8 conversion, and visit heldmotorsports for the suspention, brakes and stuff.  17" wheels, nice paint and mr mikes interior.  It will kick A$$. <P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||08-12-2001||05:26 PM||||Go To:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A> <P>Look in the "For Sale" area.<BR>It's called the "Purple Pavement Eater"<P>If you want to do your own, Archie can help.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Archie||08-12-2001||06:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by C5Vette:<BR><B> <BR>Not asking much am I?<P>Thanks for the help.<P>.]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, your not asking too much..... for a Budget of $50K you should be able to build 2 Fiero based "Supercars" as you have described.<P>As you've stated about the C-5 "too many people have them" My advice would be to Stay away from Ferrari and Lambo replicas. This is why.... As soon as you complete the car and it's the fastest, best handling, best looking car in town, go to the nearest show or cruise and some jerk will ignore the superior performance and all the other features of the car and say something (In his nastiest voice)like "It's a Fiero Kit Car" & you'll have to kill the SOB.<P>My recommendation?<P>Start with a 1987 GT 5 speed, get the Held suspension parts and a set of big brakes. Have someone who knows what he's doing install a Chevy LT1 MAF with the GM "Hot Cam Kit" & an Accell control system. If you choose to put a body kit on it, choose a body that is not a replica of anything else and you will assure yourself that the car will be judged on it's own merits.<P>I have a couple of other ideas to make the car an even better "Super Car" but I'm saving those ideas for my car.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
